#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084021} :activity/id #uuid "a68b3f7f-449b-5dcc-876b-2a9dda0b7af9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049060 :topic/id #uuid "d6922e01-c712-5ba1-b304-de1c9c2ec2dd" :topic/math-academy-id 742} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084021 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084514 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to solve, *without using graphs*, the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y=1 \\\\ 5x-y=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we must combine the equations into a single equation that can be solved using algebraic manipulation. One way to combine the equations is to use the **elimination method.** \n\nNotice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $1,$ and the coefficient of $y$ in the second equation is $-1.$ Since these numbers add to zero, we can *add* the two equations, *eliminate* the variable $y,$ and solve the resulting equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+\\cancel{y} & =1 \\\\ 5x-\\cancel{y} & =11 \\\\ (x+5x) & =(1+11) \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $x=2.$ However, we still need to find $y.$ To do this, we substitute $x=2$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (2)+y & =1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=2$ and $y=-1,$ which can be expressed as $(2, -1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "48f030ba-6728-565a-be53-cfb1e88abc86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8702 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22b878ba-aa9a-5218-889c-006c8dcacca7" :step/math-academy-id 143346 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084022}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084022 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083168 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082932 :question/id #uuid "06136eef-284e-548f-a57b-f400a05f2cb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-13993"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e0dbf68-2247-5a47-aac8-c16acc5e710c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050452}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496321}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the Second Variable"} :step/id #uuid "5234accd-01b6-501e-866d-11b931397534" :step/math-academy-id 143347 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084023}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084023 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082933 :question/id #uuid "17cb905e-e692-59b8-964d-c7b50482c11e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e562941a-078d-555f-aab6-c59d4c79dfba" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496235} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496287}] :knowledge-point/title "Eliminating the First Variable"} :step/id #uuid "adba60f0-2f20-5985-8599-c86e9c30f9db" :step/math-academy-id 143351 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084024}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084024 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084515 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose that we want to solve the following system of linear equations using elimination:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=1 \\\\ x-y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs it stands, adding the two equations will not eliminate any of the variables. \n\nHowever, suppose we want to eliminate the variable $x$. We begin by multiplying the second equation by $\\color{blue}-1$, which gives the following equivalent system:\n\n![](images/85/85996afab1889f8032a295f4db6bbbcbc8670a0bc12f0b64f53a3b809f111628.svg)\n\nThen, we add the two resulting equations together. This eliminates the variable $x,$ making it possible to solve for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{x}+2y & =1 \\\\ -\\cancel{x}+y & =2 \\\\ (2y+y) & =(1+2) \\\\ 3y & =3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution must have $y=1.$ However, we still need to find $x.$ To do this, we substitute $y=1$ back into one of the equations. Let's choose the second equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}x-y & =-2 \\\\ x-(1) & =-2 \\\\ x & =-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ which can be expressed as $(-1, 1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "170be181-ae77-50d7-a5f1-0a4bdcfa318e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8704 :tutorial/title "Eliminating Variables From Systems of Linear Equations"} :step/id #uuid "1c45a64c-2352-5510-9c77-6d975e32ee8a" :step/math-academy-id 143354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083170 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082934 :question/id #uuid "98645504-986a-5847-8042-32ced1cf4d14" :question/math-academy-id "e-13996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc55e7f0-598a-5244-84ef-6dff74f0c420" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496330}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Result of a Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "f6f3680b-161b-5ba4-b393-bb9b5b36236b" :step/math-academy-id 143355 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084026}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083171 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082935 :question/id #uuid "0e96914f-2f28-5fef-8a36-9cf2869b1b43" :question/math-academy-id "e-14000"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20e61a36-c5bf-598a-a07d-833611ffaee6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083780} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496322}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a System of Equations"} :step/id #uuid "b6308dc5-5a40-5abe-9f8e-91524b3b2359" :step/math-academy-id 143359}] :activity/title "Introduction to the Elimination Method" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083433}]}
